# Target C-II — paired-remainder path extends through `t<=1.8659`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` endpoint strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8659`  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited paired-remainder theorem through `1.865`; scope-safe matched all-train gap `g=0.007707`; corrected retained-mass feasibility; physical opposite-branch theorem; phase-free path saturation; diameter-path Helly hub  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_paired_remainder_path_t18659_g707.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The hostile-audited paired-remainder theorem already proves

\[
E_{pair}+F(z-2P)\le\delta+o(1),
\qquad F(s)=s-6\sin(s/6),
\]

and hence, at an energy threshold `lambda`,

\[
P_{low}\ge P-{\delta-F(z-2P)\over\lambda}-o(1).
\]

The `1.865` certificate used the older matched floor

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076.
\]

The current scope-safe theorem gives instead

\[
C_{707}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707,
\qquad
{T_{train}-q/2\over D}\ge C_{707}-o(1),
\]

at the same full-core Type-II scope.  This only decreases the adverse outer-energy ceiling

\[
\delta_{max}={U_0-C\over2},
\qquad U_0={9T\over2}-2\pi,
\]

so every inherited use is monotone-safe.

Set

\[
T={18659\over10000}=1.8659.
\]

The exact outward certificate proves

\[
U_0<2.113365,
\qquad
\boxed{\delta_{max}<0.100517},
\]

and rechecks the full retained-mass derivative box at this larger endpoint.  With

\[
z_0={827\over400}=2.0675,
\]

it gives

\[
\boxed{H(2.0675)<-9.4\times10^{-5}},
\]

so every survivor satisfies

\[
\boxed{z>2.0675-o(1)}.
\]

Choose

\[
\boxed{\lambda={611\over1250}=0.4888}.
\]

The exact physical same-branch test has squared margin

\[
\boxed{
\left(1-\delta_{max}-{\lambda\over2}\right)^2
-\left(\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}\right)
>6.6\times10^{-5}.
}
\]

Thus every selected low-energy O--O train has opposite oriented terminal branches.

For the paired-remainder functional, the adverse endpoint values are `t=T`, `z=z_0`, `kappa=0`, and `delta=delta_max`, exactly as in the hostile-audited `1.865` theorem.  The certificate obtains

\[
P_0>0.15516,
\qquad
z_0-2P_0>1.75716,
\]

\[
F(z_0-2P_0)>0.0250104,
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{P_{low}>0.00068-o(1)}.
\]

One of the at most three dominant phase windows carries more than

\[
\boxed{0.00022D-o(D)}
\]

actual O--O complete trains.  Their crosscuts form one simple complement-dual path; every path region is diameter-loaded; every actual diameter support interval contains one common hub region; and switching is one-sided at every nonhub boundary.

The global interval-endpoint budget satisfies

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2\pi D+o(D).
\]

Since

\[
{2(355/113)\over0.00022}<28560,
\]

some nonhub boundary has integer switch count at most

\[
\boxed{28559}.
\]

Its exact nonzero layer reaction is generated by one or two actual switching diameter bars, by the inherited sparse reaction theorem.

Hence the live paired-remainder path/hub/one-sided-strip normal form extends from `t<=1.865` to

\[
\boxed{t\le1.8659}.
\]

A floating optimization of the same proof architecture with `C_707` places the zero low-energy-mass endpoint near `1.8659696`.  Thus `1.8659` is close to the scalar limit of this exact endpoint substitution.  Material progress beyond it should improve the paired-remainder lower bound or exploit the finite one-sided strip itself.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only paired-remainder extension through `t<=1.8659`. C-II and Level I remain open.
